    {
      "id": "01",
      "title": "State and transition",
      "question": "How do you design a dynamic programming solution: what do you write down first?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Dynamic programming, question one. How do you design a D P solution? What do you write down before any code? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Designing a DP: what do you write down <em>first</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Write down four things. The state: what d p of i means, in one precise sentence. The transition: how a state is built from smaller ones, usually by trying each choice for the last step. The base cases: the smallest states, answered directly. And the order: compute each state after every state it reads. If you can't say the state in one sentence, you don't have one yet.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Four things, <em>before code</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "state",
                "label": "State",
                "title": "What dp[i] means",
                "body": "One precise sentence."
              },
              {
                "at": "transition",
                "label": "Transition",
                "title": "Built from smaller states",
                "body": "Try each choice for the last step."
              },
              {
                "at": "base",
                "label": "Base cases",
                "title": "The smallest states",
                "body": "Answered directly."
              },
              {
                "at": "order",
                "label": "Order",
                "title": "Readers after writers",
                "body": "Compute what a state reads first."
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "House robber: take the most money from a row of houses, never two neighbors. State: d p of i is the best total from houses zero through i. At house i, either skip it, and keep d p of i minus one, or rob it, and add its value to d p of i minus two. The first house alone gives two. The first two give the better of two and seven: seven. At index two, skipping keeps seven, robbing gives two plus nine: eleven. At index three, skipping keeps eleven, and robbing gives seven plus three, only ten. At the last house, eleven plus one: twelve. Each state reads only the two before it, so two variables replace the table.",
