    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Edit distance",
      "question": "How do you compute the edit distance between two strings?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. How do you compute the edit distance between two strings: the fewest inserts, deletes, and replacements? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "How do you compute <em>edit distance</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "The state takes two indices: d p of i, j is the fewest edits to turn the first i characters of one word into the first j of the other. Look at the last characters. If they match, they cost nothing: take the diagonal, d p of i minus one, j minus one. Otherwise, pay one, plus the cheapest of three moves: delete, from the cell above. Insert, from the cell to the left. Or replace, from the diagonal.",
          "props": {
            "title": "dp[i][j]: <em>prefix to prefix</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "indices",
                "label": "State",
                "title": "First i chars → first j chars",
                "body": "Fewest edits between the two prefixes.",
                "code": "dp[i][j]"
              },
              {
                "at": "match",
                "label": "Last chars match",
                "title": "Free: take the diagonal",
                "body": "",
                "code": "dp[i−1][j−1]"
              },
              {
                "at": "Otherwise",
                "label": "They differ",
                "title": "1 + the cheapest move",
                "body": "Delete: above. Insert: left. Replace: diagonal.",
                "code": "1 + min(↑, ←, ↖)"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Horse into ross. The first row and column are free: turning a prefix into nothing takes i deletes, and building a prefix from nothing takes j inserts. Now h against r: they differ, so one plus the smallest neighbor, zero on the diagonal. One: replace h with r. Next, o against o: they match, so copy the diagonal, one. The rest of the table fills the same way, row by row, each cell reading three finished neighbors. The bottom-right cell is the answer: three. Replace h with r, delete r, delete e.",
