        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Bursting a balloon earns its value times its current left and right neighbors. If you choose the first balloon to burst, its neighbors become adjacent, so the left part and the right part keep affecting each other: no clean subproblems. Choose the last one instead. When k bursts last in the range between i and j, everything between them is already gone, so its neighbors are exactly i and j. And the two sides were solved independently. That gives d p of i, j: the best of d p of i, k, plus i times k times j, plus d p of k, j.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Pick the <em>last</em> to burst",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "first",
                "label": "First to burst",
                "title": "Sides stay coupled",
                "body": "Its neighbors become adjacent; the halves interact."
              },
              {
                "at": "instead",
                "label": "Last to burst",
                "title": "Neighbors are i and j",
                "body": "Everything between is already gone."
              },
              {
                "at": "gives",
                "label": "Recurrence",
                "title": "Split at k",
                "body": "Two independent sides, plus burst(k) = n[i]·n[k]·n[j].",
                "code": "dp[i][k] + burst(k) + dp[k][j]"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
